{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014dCZe5qd1Zzcai4MhPXeEx","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information. Let me compile a complete report with all requirements, their descriptions, and weights for both SRDs.\n\n## Complete SRD Requirements Report\n\n### **SRD001 - testutils: Differential Testing Harness**\n\n| R-ID | Description | Weight |\n|------|-------------|--------|\n| **R1: Test case data structure** | | |\n| R1.1 | Define DiffTest as a named struct with Name, Args, Stdin, Env, WorkDir, ExitCode, Normalize, and ExpectedFiles fields | 1 |\n| R1.2 | DiffTest.Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately; empty slice []byte{} is semantically distinct | 1 |\n| R1.3 | DiffTest.Env nil inherits test process environment with defaults; non-nil KEY=VALUE pairs merge into inherited environment | 1 |\n| R1.4 | Define NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte as a named type alias; Normalize field uses this type | 1 |\n| **R2: Binary execution** | | |\n| R2.1 | Provide RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that runs each DiffTest as a named subtest | 1 |\n| R2.2 | Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary independently | 1 |\n| R2.3 | Impose configurable timeout on each binary invocation with 10-second default; fail if either exceeds timeout | 1 |\n| R2.4 | Must not write any output to test process stdout or stderr during a passing test run | 1 |\n| R2.5 | Support passing working directory via WorkDir field; empty = per-test t.TempDir(); non-empty = use specified directory for both binaries | 1 |\n| R2.6 | Set LC_ALL=C in environment for both binaries unless DiffTest.Env explicitly overrides it | 1 |\n| **R3: Comparison and reporting** | | |\n| R3.1 | Apply DiffTest.Normalize function (if non-nil) to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison | 1 |\n| R3.2 | Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte; any difference must be reported as test failure | 1 |\n| R3.3 | Compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte; any difference must be reported as test failure | 1 |\n| R3.4 | Compare exit codes exactly; any difference must be reported as test failure | 1 |\n| R3.5 | Report divergence with: args, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), reference stdout, Go stdout, reference stderr, Go stderr, both exit codes | 1 |\n| R3.6 | Run each DiffTest as a named subtest (t.Run) for identifiable failures in go test output | 1 |\n| **R4: Normalization hooks** | | |\n| R4.1 | Allow per-DiffTest normalization via Normalize field; function receives raw bytes and returns normalized bytes | 1 |\n| R4.2 | Provide built-in TimestampNormalizer that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with fixed placeholder string | 1 |\n| R4.3 | Allow normalizers to be composed; harness accepts slice of Normalize functions applied in order (nil/empty = no normalization) | 1 |\n| R4.4 | Provide ComposeNormalizers(fns ...NormalizeFunc) NormalizeFunc that returns single NormalizeFunc applying functions in order | 1 |\n| **R5: File-state comparison** | | |\n| R5.1 | Support utilities with primary file output (sponge, cp, mv) via DiffTest.ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte for file paths and expected content | 1 |\n| R5.2 | Compare each ExpectedFiles entry byte-for-byte against Go binary output; report missing files and differences as failures | 1 |\n\n---\n\n### **SRD003 - format: Output Formatting**\n\n| R-ID | Description | Weight |\n|------|-------------|--------|\n| **R1: Column alignment** | | |\n| R1.1 | Provide Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into max columns fitting termWidth | 1 |\n| R1.2 | Provide PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string functions for field padding | 1 |\n| R1.3 | Treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly; column width from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count | 1 |\n| R1.4 | Utility context: ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in terminal width using longest filename in each column | 1 |\n| **R2: ANSI color** | | |\n| R2.1 | Provide FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function returning ANSI escape sequence for file type (dir, symlink, executable, block/char device, socket, pipe, regular) | 1 |\n| R2.2 | Provide Reset() string constant or function returning ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\") | 1 |\n| R2.3 | Provide ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function returning true only when w is a TTY; uses pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) for detection | 1 |\n| R2.4 | Use GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block/char device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (default) | 1 |\n| R2.5 | Utility context: ls outputs ANSI escapes before/after each filename in colorized mode; suppressed when stdout is not TTY or --color=never | 1 |\n| R2.6 | Provide SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) function overriding automatic TTY detection globally; true = always colorize, false = never colorize | 1 |\n| R2.7 | Provide ResetColorEnabled() function clearing SetColorEnabled override and reverting to automatic TTY detection; utilities must call in deferred cleanup | 1 |\n| **R3: Human-readable size conversion** | | |\n| R3.1 | Provide HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string function with HumanSizeOpts struct {Binary bool} field | 1 |\n| R3.2 | When opts.Binary true (default), use base 1024 with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E; false = base 1000 with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB | 1 |\n| R3.3 | Format output to at most one decimal place when value is not integer at chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes = \"1.5K\", not \"2K\") | 1 |\n| R3.4 | Return \"0\" for zero byte count regardless of unit mode | 1 |\n| R3.5 | Utility context: ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output; same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts) | 1 |\n| R3.6 | Utility context: du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings; du uses same binary/SI distinction as ls -h | 1 |\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**SRD001 (testutils):**\n- Total Requirements: 22 (R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.6, R3.1-R3.6, R4.1-R4.4, R5.1-R5.2)\n- All weights: 1\n- Status breakdown: R1.1-R2.4 complete (8), R2.5-R5.2 uncertain (14)\n\n**SRD003 (format):**\n- Total Requirements: 16 (R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.7, R3.1-R3.6)\n- All weights: 1\n- Status breakdown: R1.1-R3.4 complete (13), R3.5-R3.6 uncertain (2) in the first reference; then R1.1-R4.3 ready (various) in a second reference set\n\nAll requirements across both SRDs have a weight of **1**, indicating uniform weighting with no differentiation between requirements."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c01ef8fc-8a66-448d-af06-27f5898a2cc4","uuid":"aa190c2d-1323-4556-9c2b-69d328cc28e2","timestamp":"2026-05-14T01:59:21.797Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Find and read the SRD files for testutils (srd001) and format (srd003) in the docs/specs/software-requirements/ directory. I need to see all requirements with their IDs, text, and weight fields. Also check if there's a requirements.yaml file that might contain weights separately. The project is at /Users/pdiddy/COBBLER/generation-run44/. Report back the full list of requirements (R-IDs, descriptions, and weights) for both SRDs.","agentId":"a0d48af0c2b0ae90d","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information. Let me compile a complete report with all requirements, their descriptions, and weights for both SRDs.\n\n## Complete SRD Requirements Report\n\n### **SRD001 - testutils: Differential Testing Harness**\n\n| R-ID | Description | Weight |\n|------|-------------|--------|\n| **R1: Test case data structure** | | |\n| R1.1 | Define DiffTest as a named struct with Name, Args, Stdin, Env, WorkDir, ExitCode, Normalize, and ExpectedFiles fields | 1 |\n| R1.2 | DiffTest.Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately; empty slice []byt